<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ladenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lopodunum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ladenburg is a small town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. As Roman Lopodunum it was the chief town of a district on the Neckar. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Worms, who from the 14th century shared its lordship with the Electoral Palatinate. The motor pioneer Carl Benz spent his last years there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ladenburg.jpg" alt="Ladenburg" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tilman2007 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ladenburg,_Marktplatz_7,_St._Gallus_Kirche_und_Marktplatz_6_20170601_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
